1	//! Asking a checker for the component a session was just asked about (ADR 0022).
2	//!
3	//! The structural answer is always computed first and is a complete answer on its own. With a [`SemanticProvider`]
4	//! attached, [`SemanticHook::refine`] asks the checker for exactly that component, waits a bounded time, and merges
5	//! its props over the structural ones. A checker that is slower than the budget leaves the structural answer in
6	//! place, and its late answer is kept for the next request of the same, unedited component.
7	//!
8	//! An answer describes the component's props as the checker read them, which depends on every file the component
9	//! imports, not only its own. So the session drops every answer when any source file changes
10	//! ([`SemanticHook::invalidate`]), and an answer that was still on its way then is not kept. The same call tells the
11	//! checker which files changed, since a warm one answers from what it read before (ADR 0026 S6).
12	
13	use std::hash::{Hash, Hasher};
14	use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicBool, Ordering};
15	use std::sync::mpsc::{Receiver, RecvTimeoutError};
16	use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex, PoisonError};
17	use std::time::Duration;
18	
19	use rustc_hash::{FxHashMap, FxHasher};
20	
21	use super::ComponentAnswer;
22	use crate::pipeline::HtmlAttributeMode;
23	use crate::react_types::{checker_prop_used, react_props_wanted};
24	use crate::types::diagnostic::{Diagnostic, DiagnosticCode, DiagnosticSeverity};
25	use crate::types::semantic::{
26	    MergedProps, SemanticComponent, SemanticFallback, SemanticOutcome, SemanticProp, SemanticProvider, SemanticTarget,
